    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Salmon-pink bathroom tiles, a speckled brown kitchen splashback, a cracked beige floor: dated tiles are one of the most common complaints about rented homes, and one of the hardest to fix when you can’t drill, regrout or retile. You can cover ugly rental tile temporarily, though, as long as you’re realistic about what heat, steam and splashing will do to stick-on products.</p>
<p>This guide covers removable options for kitchen and bathroom walls and floors, where each one works (and where it doesn’t), and how to test so you get your deposit back.</p>

<h2 id="before-you-start">Before you start: protect your deposit</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Read your tenancy agreement.</strong> Some agreements say no adhesive products on walls or fixtures. If in doubt, ask your landlord or letting agent and get their answer in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph the tiles</strong> before you cover them, alongside your check-in inventory, so there’s no dispute about their original condition.</li>
<li><strong>Clean and dry the surface thoroughly.</strong> Most stick-on products fail because of grease or soap residue. Use a degreaser in the kitchen and a soap-scum remover in the bathroom, rinse, and let everything dry fully.</li>
<li><strong>Patch test in a hidden spot</strong>, such as behind the toilet or inside a cupboard end, for at least one to two weeks of normal use, including cooking or showers. Then peel it off and check for residue, lifted glaze or pulled grout.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="peel-and-stick-tile-covers">Peel-and-stick tile covers for walls</h2>
<p>Peel-and-stick tiles and tile-effect panels are thin vinyl or composite sheets with an adhesive backing. They go straight over existing glazed tile and can change a splashback or bathroom wall in an afternoon.</p>
<h3>Where they work</h3>
<ul>
<li>Kitchen splashbacks <strong>away from the hob</strong>, such as behind the sink or along a run of worktop.</li>
<li>Bathroom walls outside the shower, for example behind the basin or toilet, or a half-height tiled area.</li>
<li>Smooth, flat, glazed tiles with narrow, level grout lines.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Where they struggle</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Directly behind a hob.</strong> Most vinyl products aren’t designed for high heat and can warp, discolour or lift. Check the product’s heat rating and your hob manufacturer’s guidance. If you’re unsure, leave the area behind the hob uncovered or use a freestanding, heat-resistant panel recommended for that purpose.</li>
<li><strong>Inside showers and around baths</strong> where water sits on the wall for long periods. Even products sold as waterproof can lift at the edges, and water trapped behind them can lead to mould.</li>
<li><strong>Textured, uneven or deeply grouted tiles.</strong> Wide grout lines can show through as ridges and leave air pockets that fail early.</li>
</ul>
<h3>How to fit them neatly</h3>
<ul>
<li>Start at the most visible point, usually the centre of the splashback, so any trimmed pieces end up in corners.</li>
<li>Peel back only a few centimetres of backing at a time and press from the centre outwards with a felt-edged smoother.</li>
<li>Leave a hairline gap at worktops and basins and, where the manufacturer allows, finish with a removable, low-tack sealant rather than permanent silicone.</li>
<li>Trim around sockets with the power off, and never cover sockets, switches or isolator switches.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="tile-stickers-and-grout">Tile stickers and grout: covering small areas</h2>
<p>If the tiles themselves are fine but a few are dated, individual tile stickers (decals) are gentler than full covers. Choose stickers cut slightly smaller than the tile so they sit <strong>inside</strong> the grout lines. They look more like real tile, stick to the smooth glaze rather than the porous grout, and are much easier to remove later.</p>
<p>Avoid stickers that cross grout lines in wet areas. Moisture creeps into the grout underneath and the edges start to curl. Grout pens and grout paint are a different thing: they’re semi-permanent, so treat them as a change to the property and ask permission first.</p>

<h2 id="washi-tape-caveats">Washi tape and contact paper: the caveats</h2>
<p>Washi tape patterns and contact paper show up a lot in rental makeover photos. They can work for a short-term refresh on a <strong>dry</strong> wall, such as a tiled alcove away from the sink, but keep these limits in mind:</p>
<ul>
<li>They aren’t designed for steam, splashes or heat and tend to peel, bubble or fade in kitchens and bathrooms.</li>
<li>Some leave a sticky residue after months on the wall, especially in warm rooms.</li>
<li>Paper-based products absorb water and can stain or trap moisture against grout.</li>
</ul>
<p>Think of them as a temporary styling trick, not a long-term cover.</p>

<h2 id="floor-tiles">Covering floor tiles</h2>
<p>Floors get foot traffic, water and furniture dragged over them, so stick-on floor stickers often lift at the edges within months. These options are more reliable:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Loose-lay vinyl sheet.</strong> Cut to size and laid flat without glue, it covers a whole bathroom or kitchen floor and lifts out when you leave. Choose one with a grippy backing, and cut it to fit tightly around the toilet and units so it doesn’t shift.</li>
<li><strong>Interlocking click vinyl tiles or planks</strong> designed to float over existing tile. Check the product suits wet rooms and that it won’t stop doors opening; you may need clearance of a few millimetres.</li>
<li><strong>Rugs and runners.</strong> A washable cotton runner along a galley kitchen, or a large bath mat that covers most of a small bathroom floor, hides a lot of tile. Use a non-slip underlay or a mat designed for wet floors, because slips on tiles are a real hazard.</li>
<li><strong>Check rubber backings.</strong> Some rubber or latex-backed mats can discolour certain floor finishes over time. Test in a corner, and lift and air mats regularly so moisture doesn’t get trapped underneath.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Measure the full floor and leave a 1 cm (⅜ in) gap around the edge when cutting loose-lay vinyl. Floors and walls are rarely square, and a tiny gap stops the sheet buckling as the room warms and cools.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="removal">How to remove covers at the end of your tenancy</h2>
<ul>
<li>Warm the adhesive gently with a hairdryer on a low setting so it softens.</li>
<li>Peel slowly at a low angle, folding the cover back on itself rather than pulling it straight out from the wall.</li>
<li>Remove leftover adhesive with warm soapy water first, then, if needed, an adhesive remover suitable for tiles and grout, tested on a hidden spot first.</li>
<li>Allow time. Leave yourself a few days before check-out in case a stubborn patch needs a second go.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="styling-to-distract">Renter-safe styling that distracts from tiles</h2>
<p>Sometimes the best approach is to work with the tiles instead of hiding them. A few styling moves can make even the most stubborn colour look deliberate.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Pick a palette that suits the tile.</strong> Pink or peach tiles look intentional with terracotta, olive green and cream. Green or avocado tiles work with warm wood, mustard and white. Fighting the tile with a clashing colour only draws attention to it.</li>
<li><strong>Add a bold shower curtain</strong> or fabric blind. A large pattern becomes the focal point and covers a big area of wall without a single fixing.</li>
<li><strong>Bring in towels, bath mats and storage in one colour family</strong> so the room looks considered, not cluttered.</li>
<li><strong>Use freestanding storage.</strong> An over-toilet shelving unit, a ladder shelf or a slim trolley adds wood or metal finishes and covers some wall tile without drilling.</li>
<li><strong>Choose humidity-loving plants</strong> for a bright bathroom windowsill. Greenery breaks up the expanse of tile beautifully.</li>
<li><strong>Prop art rather than hang it.</strong> Adhesive strips are unreliable in steamy rooms, so lean small framed prints on a shelf, windowsill or cistern, away from direct splashes.</li>
<li><strong>Improve the lighting.</strong> A warm, diffused light is far kinder to dated tiles than a harsh cool-white bulb. Any change to bathroom light fittings must be done by a qualified electrician with the landlord’s permission.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="quick-guide">Quick guide: which option where?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Kitchen splashback away from the hob:</strong> peel-and-stick tile panels, tested first.</li>
<li><strong>Behind the hob:</strong> leave uncovered unless a product is specifically rated for that heat. Check the manufacturer’s guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Bathroom walls outside the shower:</strong> peel-and-stick panels or tile decals within the grout lines.</li>
<li><strong>Inside the shower:</strong> avoid stick-on covers. Style the rest of the room instead.</li>
<li><strong>Floors:</strong> loose-lay vinyl, floating click tiles or large non-slip rugs.</li>
</ul>

